@@s001 Code: knapsack in Java
@@s002 Code: knapsack in Java
@@s003 The knapsack table opt[i][w] in Java: filling row by row, and the traceback to reconstruct the chosen objects.
@@s004 The table
@@s005 The table has <code>(n + 1) × (W + 1)</code> cells. Row 0 and column 0 are the base cases: Java initialises them to 0 by itself.
@@s006 The table
@@s007 Two-dimensional array of <code>int</code>: each cell starts at 0.
@@s008 First case of the recurrence: I skip object i, so the value is the one with i − 1 objects.
@@s009 The second case exists only if the object fits in the budget.
@@s010 The maximum between skipping it and taking it: <code>value + OPT(i − 1, w − weight)</code>.
@@s011 Syntax notes
@@s012 <code>Math.max(a, b)</code> is the library function: there is no need to write the <code>if</code> by hand.
@@s013 The outer loop on <code>i</code> and the inner one on <code>w</code> respect the order of computation: each cell depends only on rows already computed.
@@s014 Traceback: which objects
@@s015 I start from the final cell and go back up. If the cell differs from the row above, object i was taken and the budget goes down.
@@s016 Traceback: which objects
@@s017 List of the chosen objects. It must be reversed at the end because I find them from the last one.
@@s018 If the value changes, object i is in the solution.
@@s019 I remove it from the budget: the rest of the choice is a smaller subproblem.
@@s020 I reverse the list, so the objects come out in increasing order.
@@s021 Syntax notes
@@s022 <code>Collections</code> is in <code>java.util</code>: <code>reverse</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>max</code> are utilities for lists.
@@s023 Running it
@@s024 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
